00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:07.000
this benchmark came in the picture because

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.000
MMLU was saturated

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:16.000
and then people thought that what did we do in MMLU?

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.000
we checked the breadth of knowledge

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:24.000
by asking questions around 57 subjects

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.000
but those questions were mostly easy

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:32.000
if you go and see that data set yourself, you will realize that the questions are not very difficult

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:35.000
you can also answer

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.000
so here some researchers thought that

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:41.000
it is possible that as LLM gets smarter

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.000
it will be very easy to answer easy questions for them

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.000
so what if rather than checking the breadth of knowledge

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:53.000
what if we check the depth of knowledge?

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.000
basically rather than asking simple questions

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:59.000
what if we ask difficult questions?

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.000
like really difficult questions

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.000
PhD level questions

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.000
but the problem is that if you go in depth

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:11.000
then it is not possible to cover a lot of subjects

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:14.000
because obviously expertise is costly in the world

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:20.000
so they thought that rather than going all out in 57 subjects

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.000
let's just focus on 3 subjects

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.000
which 3 subjects?

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.000
physics, chemistry and bio

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.000
and we will make a science based benchmark

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.000
which will be made with research level PhD questions

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.000
and the specialty of these questions is that

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:43.000
if a non-specialist comes from any other domain

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:47.000
then even if he gets Google, we give him 30 minutes of time

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.000
then he will not be able to solve even one question

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:53.000
questions of this level will be put in this data set

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:56.000
see everything is written here

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:00.000
it was launched in November 2023 and it was called Google proof

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.000
PhD people made this benchmark by adding the hardest questions

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:08.000
of science as in physics, chemistry and biology

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
and the biggest differentiator of this was that

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.000
every question that was put in this data set

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.000
was validated by 2 domain experts

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.000
so it was not a mistake

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:23.000
and these questions are so difficult

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:26.000
that even if a non-expert comes

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:29.000
and he gets Google access

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:32.000
then he will not be able to solve even one question in 30 minutes

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:35.000
and we call this Google proof

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.000
and this is the part of its name

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.000
if we tell the current status of this benchmark

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:44.000
now it is 2026 so this benchmark is

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:47.000
near-scientific

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:50.000
so now this benchmark is near-saturation

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.000
now the new generation LLMs

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:56.000
which have reasoning capability

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.000
and have been trained on more data

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:02.000
now they are going above 80% in this benchmark

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:05.000
so you can say that

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.000
in a few years it will be saturated

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.000
so here the same lineage is told

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.000
that in November 2023 GPQA was released

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.000
and the most state of the art model of that time was GPT-4

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:20.000
which could only solve 39% of the questions

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:23.000
of the main data set

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:26.000
after that in 2024

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.000
GPT-4O came

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.000
which reached 56% in the diamond data set

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:35.000
so there are actually 3 data sets in GPQA

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.000
one is the normal one

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:41.000
one is, I don't remember the name

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.000
one is the main one

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:47.000
one is the extended one

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:50.000
and one is the diamond one

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.000
so in the main one there are

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:56.000
443 questions, in the extended one

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.000
there are 546 questions

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.000
and in the diamond one there are only 198 questions

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.000
so in the extended one

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.000
there are all the questions

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.000
and then they edited this

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:14.000
so in some questions

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.000
they saw errors

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:20.000
so after removing those questions, there were 443 questions left

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.000
and in these 443 questions

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:26.000
198 most difficult questions were put in this

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.000
diamond data set

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.000
so whenever someone says that in GPQA we achieved 63%

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:35.000
or 83%

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.000
it means that how much percent they achieved

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.000
in the most difficult data set

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:44.000
so here it is written exactly that

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.000
in 2024 GPT-4O came

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:50.000
and it scored 56% in the diamond one

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:53.000
then came the O1 of OpenAI

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:56.000
which was a very strong reasoning model of 2024

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:59.000
it achieved 78%

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:02.000
at this point, OpenAI

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:05.000
hired experts and gave them

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:08.000
this data set to solve

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:11.000
so those experts solved 69.7%

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:14.000
or their score came

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:17.000
so at this point OpenAI did a lot of marketing

