01:13:45.000 --> 01:13:48.000
it is written in front

01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:51.000
it is very simple

01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:54.000
what are benchmarks

01:13:54.000 --> 01:13:57.000
benchmarks are an exam to test LLMs in a particular aspect

01:13:57.000 --> 01:14:00.000
so it is an exam

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:03.000
after giving the exam result comes

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:06.000
you will publish the result somewhere

01:14:06.000 --> 01:14:09.000
somewhere you will show

01:14:09.000 --> 01:14:12.000
that place is your leader votes

01:14:12.000 --> 01:14:15.000
like leader votes in your school

01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:18.000
who tell who topped or whatever

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:21.000
so here is a simple definition

01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:24.000
and LLM leader vote is a public ranking

01:14:24.000 --> 01:14:27.000
that shows how different LLMs

01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:30.000
perform on a common set of evaluations

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:33.000
so benchmark result

01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:36.000
we show on the leader vote

01:14:36.000 --> 01:14:39.000
and leader vote is a single place where we can compare

01:14:39.000 --> 01:14:42.000
different models on one benchmark

01:14:42.000 --> 01:14:45.000
so that we get an overview

01:14:45.000 --> 01:14:48.000
which is the best model in this benchmark

01:14:48.000 --> 01:14:51.000
that is the basic idea of a leader vote

01:14:51.000 --> 01:14:54.000
why do leader votes exist

01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:57.000
one reason I just told you

01:14:57.000 --> 01:15:00.000
that we compare models across labs with a common reference

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:03.000
same exam given by all the models

01:15:03.000 --> 01:15:06.000
who came top, who came second, who came last

01:15:06.000 --> 01:15:09.000
we get to know that from that

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:12.000
who we have to use in our application

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:15.000
second, leader votes also provide trust

01:15:15.000 --> 01:15:18.000
generally leader votes are third party

01:15:18.000 --> 01:15:21.000
so if Claude himself says that

01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:24.000
we scored so much on this benchmark

01:15:24.000 --> 01:15:27.000
then you will not trust him that much

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:30.000
because obviously openAI will want more praise for his model

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:33.000
but if a third party comes and says that we have tested Claude

01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:36.000
on one exam and also openAI

01:15:36.000 --> 01:15:39.000
and these are the results, then you will believe more

01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:42.000
because it is a third party, its stakes are not as high

01:15:42.000 --> 01:15:45.000
as openAI and Claude

01:15:45.000 --> 01:15:47.000
so we have to use that

01:15:47.000 --> 01:15:50.000
second, it guides model selection

01:15:50.000 --> 01:15:53.000
when you don't have resources to run

01:15:53.000 --> 01:15:56.000
evals yourself

01:15:56.000 --> 01:15:59.000
you want to make a chart board

01:15:59.000 --> 01:16:02.000
which has the capabilities of maths

01:16:02.000 --> 01:16:05.000
your students are like this

01:16:05.000 --> 01:16:08.000
so obviously you want me to test the evaluation

01:16:08.000 --> 01:16:11.000
but is it possible for you

01:16:11.000 --> 01:16:14.000
that you can do hundreds of models

01:16:14.000 --> 01:16:17.000
and you want to test all of them

01:16:17.000 --> 01:16:20.000
its not possible, there are so many models

01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:23.000
you have to select one model

01:16:23.000 --> 01:16:26.000
so what is your job, that you bring 100 models

01:16:26.000 --> 01:16:29.000
and run those evaluations on 100

01:16:29.000 --> 01:16:32.000
it will take a lot of money

01:16:32.000 --> 01:16:35.000
it will take a lot of money

01:16:35.000 --> 01:16:38.000
it will take a lot of time and effort

01:16:38.000 --> 01:16:41.000
if someone else is doing it for you, then its easy for you

01:16:41.000 --> 01:16:44.000
so you can simply go to the leaderboard

01:16:44.000 --> 01:16:47.000
and pick your top 10 and pick your model

01:16:47.000 --> 01:16:50.000
so this is the second benefit

01:16:50.000 --> 01:16:53.000
third, with the help of this we get to know

01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:56.000
that has any benchmark saturated

01:16:56.000 --> 01:16:59.000
if a lot of top models

01:16:59.000 --> 01:17:02.000
start to cluster around the same score

01:17:02.000 --> 01:17:05.000
there is a benchmark that tests knowledge capability

01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:08.000
there the score of top 10 LLMs

01:17:08.000 --> 01:17:11.000
and its score is between 92-94

01:17:11.000 --> 01:17:14.000
so as soon as the clustering started to appear on the LLM leaderboard

01:17:14.000 --> 01:17:17.000
we understood that this benchmark is saturating

01:17:17.000 --> 01:17:20.000
so that is also a purpose for which people use LLM leaderboards

01:17:20.000 --> 01:17:23.000
and the fourth one is also very useful

01:17:23.000 --> 01:17:26.000
I have been working on this since 2022

01:17:26.000 --> 01:17:29.000
that whenever I have to discover new models

01:17:29.000 --> 01:17:32.000
