why they exist and who takes their work

01:22:20.000 --> 01:22:23.000
now let's discuss quickly

01:22:23.000 --> 01:22:26.000
what are the different types of leaderboards that exist

01:22:26.000 --> 01:22:29.000
so precisely there are 4 types of leaderboards

01:22:29.000 --> 01:22:32.000
which you will get to see

01:22:32.000 --> 01:22:35.000
the first and simplest leaderboard is

01:22:35.000 --> 01:22:38.000
benchmark specific leaderboards

01:22:38.000 --> 01:22:41.000
so here your leaderboard

01:22:41.000 --> 01:22:44.000
creates a ranking on the basis of a single benchmark

01:22:44.000 --> 01:22:47.000
it is written here

01:22:47.000 --> 01:22:50.000
these rank models using the result of one particular benchmark

01:22:50.000 --> 01:22:53.000
either it is MMLU or HumanEval

01:22:53.000 --> 01:22:56.000
for coding or it is GSM8 for maths

01:22:56.000 --> 01:22:59.000
or it is GPQ

01:22:59.000 --> 01:23:02.000
so you will see all the models on a single benchmark

01:23:02.000 --> 01:23:05.000
and that ranking will be shown in the form of a leaderboard

01:23:05.000 --> 01:23:08.000
so the main idea is to tell which model

01:23:08.000 --> 01:23:11.000
performs best on this benchmark

01:23:11.000 --> 01:23:14.000
the only problem with this kind of leaderboard is

01:23:14.000 --> 01:23:17.000
that they give you a very narrow view

01:23:17.000 --> 01:23:20.000
how a model performs on a single benchmark

01:23:20.000 --> 01:23:23.000
you just get to know this, how is the overall model

01:23:23.000 --> 01:23:26.000
you don't get an idea about it, this is a very good example

