they have a separate leaderboard for coding agents

01:26:23.000 --> 01:26:26.000
speech, image, audio, hardware

01:26:26.000 --> 01:26:29.000
so a lot of leaderboards are there

01:26:29.000 --> 01:26:32.000
then they have made an overall by accumulating everything

01:26:32.000 --> 01:26:35.000
so this is the most useful category

01:26:35.000 --> 01:26:38.000
personally I use this the most and people use it the most

01:26:41.000 --> 01:26:44.000
this type of leaderboards answer this question

01:26:44.000 --> 01:26:47.000
which model provides the strongest overall combination of capability, cost and performance

01:26:47.000 --> 01:26:50.000
this is the leaderboard that you will use the most

01:26:50.000 --> 01:26:53.000
third is another interesting category

01:26:53.000 --> 01:26:56.000
this is called human preference based leaderboards

01:26:56.000 --> 01:26:59.000
in this you don't make a rank based on a benchmark

01:26:59.000 --> 01:27:02.000
what you do is

01:27:02.000 --> 01:27:05.000
you make a comparison

01:27:05.000 --> 01:27:08.000
so your user comes to your website

01:27:08.000 --> 01:27:11.000
you ask him a question

01:27:11.000 --> 01:27:14.000
same question is asked by two models

01:27:14.000 --> 01:27:17.000
A and B

01:27:17.000 --> 01:27:20.000
both the answers are there

01:27:20.000 --> 01:27:23.000
now you ask the user whether you liked A or B

01:27:23.000 --> 01:27:26.000
so basically you are scoring yourself

01:27:26.000 --> 01:27:29.000
on the basis of helpfulness, clarity, writing quality, creativity

01:27:29.000 --> 01:27:32.000
and the way of giving the answer

01:27:32.000 --> 01:27:35.000
and when a lot of these types of votes are collected

01:27:35.000 --> 01:27:38.000
then a ranking is made on the basis of that

01:27:38.000 --> 01:27:41.000
and that ranking is shown in this leaderboard

01:27:41.000 --> 01:27:44.000
here it is written

01:27:44.000 --> 01:27:47.000
these rank models using human votes

