01:32:08.000 --> 01:32:11.000
but you cannot blindly trust leaderboards

01:32:11.000 --> 01:32:14.000
this is very important

01:32:14.000 --> 01:32:17.000
because you will work like AI engineers in the future

01:32:17.000 --> 01:32:20.000
and most of the times when you go to make an application

01:32:20.000 --> 01:32:23.000
then step one is model selection

01:32:23.000 --> 01:32:26.000
and in model selection also step one is to go to the leaderboards

01:32:26.000 --> 01:32:29.000
and see which model is at the top

01:32:29.000 --> 01:32:32.000
so we get the bias that if it is on the leaderboard then it will be right

01:32:32.000 --> 01:32:35.000
but it is not, you cannot blindly trust leaderboards

01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:38.000
what are the reasons we are discussing

01:32:38.000 --> 01:32:41.000
the first reason is that benchmark performance may not

01:32:41.000 --> 01:32:44.000
transfer to real applications

01:32:44.000 --> 01:32:47.000
this is very important

01:32:47.000 --> 01:32:50.000
if a model is hitting 80-90

01:32:50.000 --> 01:32:53.000
it does not mean that it will give you the same results

01:32:53.000 --> 01:32:56.000
in your real application

01:32:56.000 --> 01:32:59.000
like the famous thing that if you solve the problem on Kaggle

01:32:59.000 --> 01:33:02.000
it does not mean that you will become a good data scientist in real life

01:33:02.000 --> 01:33:05.000
why was it said like that?

01:33:05.000 --> 01:33:08.000
because the data that is given on Kaggle is very clean

01:33:08.000 --> 01:33:11.000
problem statement is very clear

01:33:11.000 --> 01:33:14.000
generally you get easy work, everything is messy in real world

01:33:14.000 --> 01:33:17.000
benchmark data is generally clean

01:33:17.000 --> 01:33:20.000
and models will perform well on it

01:33:20.000 --> 01:33:23.000
in real world there are many things like ambiguous requests

01:33:23.000 --> 01:33:26.000
missing information, company specific data, tool failure

01:33:26.000 --> 01:33:29.000
unusual edge cases

01:33:29.000 --> 01:33:32.000
whether the model handles all these or not, we don't know

01:33:32.000 --> 01:33:35.000
so yeah, this is the first reason

01:33:35.000 --> 01:33:38.000
second, as we discussed

01:33:38.000 --> 01:33:41.000
benchmarks are easily contaminated

01:33:41.000 --> 01:33:44.000
if the model is contaminated, then their leaderboard score will be contaminated

01:33:44.000 --> 01:33:47.000
and inflated, it will be seen more

01:33:47.000 --> 01:33:50.000
so don't blindly trust

01:33:50.000 --> 01:33:53.000
it is possible that the score shown on the leaderboard is genuine

01:33:53.000 --> 01:33:56.000
but it is also possible that the model has memorized

01:33:56.000 --> 01:33:59.000
or it already knew these kinds of questions

01:33:59.000 --> 01:34:02.000
so you cannot trust blindly

01:34:02.000 --> 01:34:05.000
after that, this is a very genuine problem

01:34:05.000 --> 01:34:08.000
and people have started discussing this in the last 1-2 years

01:34:08.000 --> 01:34:11.000
so it is very clear that models can be over optimized

01:34:11.000 --> 01:34:14.000
for the leaderboard

01:34:14.000 --> 01:34:17.000
sometimes a particular leaderboard becomes very popular

01:34:17.000 --> 01:34:20.000
like LM Erina

01:34:20.000 --> 01:34:23.000
it has become very popular that who is top on LM Erina

01:34:23.000 --> 01:34:26.000
so now companies also see

01:34:26.000 --> 01:34:29.000
that the most important thing is who is top on LM Erina

01:34:29.000 --> 01:34:32.000
so we have to top

01:34:32.000 --> 01:34:35.000
now who will top on LM Erina? who will make humans top

01:34:35.000 --> 01:34:38.000
so we will format it in this way

01:34:38.000 --> 01:34:41.000
we will give soft answers

01:34:41.000 --> 01:34:44.000
we will give some shabby answers

01:34:44.000 --> 01:34:47.000
so there are more chances that our model will be selected

01:34:47.000 --> 01:34:50.000
so now they will feed this kind of data

01:34:50.000 --> 01:34:53.000
in their training or fine tuning stages

01:34:53.000 --> 01:34:56.000
so that rather than

01:34:56.000 --> 01:34:59.000
perform well in actual capability

01:34:59.000 --> 01:35:02.000
perform well in situations like LM Erina

01:35:02.000 --> 01:35:05.000
so what will happen is that your model

01:35:05.000 --> 01:35:08.000
will start scoring well on the leaderboard

01:35:08.000 --> 01:35:11.000
but will not improve its real world capabilities

01:35:11.000 --> 01:35:14.000
so here is a very famous law

01:35:14.000 --> 01:35:17.000
Goodhart's law, it says

01:35:17.000 --> 01:35:20.000
when a measure becomes a target

01:35:20.000 --> 01:35:23.000
it becomes less useful as a measure

01:35:23.000 --> 01:35:26.000
so if you start targeting a metric

01:35:26.000 --> 01:35:29.000
we will improve this metric, suppose you are a car

01:35:29.000 --> 01:35:32.000
so now in India people buy cars only with mileage

01:35:32.000 --> 01:35:35.000
so now your whole engineering team is focusing

01:35:35.000 --> 01:35:38.000
on how to improve the mileage

01:35:38.000 --> 01:35:41.000
so the whole engineering will be around mileage

01:35:41.000 --> 01:35:44.000
but the overall car will get spoiled

01:35:44.000 --> 01:35:47.000
because you are not focusing on driving dynamics

01:35:47.000 --> 01:35:50.000
0-100 in how much time it reaches

01:35:50.000 --> 01:35:53.000
you are focusing only on mileage, so here it is written

01:35:53.000 --> 01:35:56.000
that if you target a metric, then that metric

01:35:56.000 --> 01:35:59.000
will not be good, so that has started happening nowadays

01:35:59.000 --> 01:36:02.000
that companies are building models

01:36:02.000 --> 01:36:05.000
so that they can top the leaderboards

01:36:05.000 --> 01:36:08.000
but the actual performance will not be good

01:36:08.000 --> 01:36:11.000
after that, this is also an important thing

01:36:11.000 --> 01:36:14.000
especially for those leaderboards

01:36:14.000 --> 01:36:17.000
which combine multiple benchmarks

01:36:17.000 --> 01:36:20.000
and show you the results

01:36:20.000 --> 01:36:23.000
now the problem there is that

01:36:23.000 --> 01:36:26.000
which benchmarks are being included

01:36:26.000 --> 01:36:29.000
which are being excluded, that is on the leaderboard

01:36:29.000 --> 01:36:32.000
after that, how the scores are normalized

01:36:32.000 --> 01:36:35.000
suppose we are calculating

01:36:35.000 --> 01:36:38.000
an overall score of different capabilities

01:36:38.000 --> 01:36:41.000
so we are not being told how much weightage

01:36:41.000 --> 01:36:44.000
we are giving to every capability, so such things

01:36:44.000 --> 01:36:47.000
are hidden and they can sometimes bother you

01:36:47.000 --> 01:36:50.000
so whenever you are looking at a leaderboard

01:36:50.000 --> 01:36:53.000
the more transparency there is, the better

01:36:53.000 --> 01:36:56.000
the data about the leaderboard is

01:36:56.000 --> 01:36:59.000
after that, in a leaderboard

01:36:59.000 --> 01:37:02.000
small differences do not matter

01:37:02.000 --> 01:37:05.000
like there are two models, one has a score of 84.3

01:37:05.000 --> 01:37:08.000
and the other is 84.1

01:37:08.000 --> 01:37:11.000
and this model came in 3rd position

01:37:11.000 --> 01:37:14.000
and this one went in 5th position

01:37:14.000 --> 01:37:17.000
but the difference was only of 0.2

01:37:17.000 --> 01:37:20.000
so we don't understand that we should choose the 3rd one

01:37:20.000 --> 01:37:23.000
we will not choose the 5th one for our application

01:37:23.000 --> 01:37:26.000
but there is a good chance that both are very similar

01:37:26.000 --> 01:37:29.000
and 5th would be better for your application

01:37:29.000 --> 01:37:32.000
so sometimes we start focusing more

01:37:32.000 --> 01:37:35.000
on which rank the models are on

01:37:35.000 --> 01:37:38.000
so the rank bias is there

01:37:38.000 --> 01:37:41.000
that is not a good thing, if the difference is less

01:37:41.000 --> 01:37:44.000
then the models are of the same level

01:37:44.000 --> 01:37:47.000
so in rank 20, there will be no difference between rank 1 and rank 25

01:37:47.000 --> 01:37:50.000
one or two questions can be wrong for anyone

01:37:50.000 --> 01:37:53.000
so that is the logic here

01:37:53.000 --> 01:37:56.000
6th is human preference leaderboards have human biases

01:37:56.000 --> 01:37:59.000
we have already discussed this, now LM arena is there

01:37:59.000 --> 01:38:02.000
now the top model here does not necessarily mean

01:38:02.000 --> 01:38:05.000
that it is objectively the top model

01:38:05.000 --> 01:38:08.000
humans like long answers, more confident answers

01:38:08.000 --> 01:38:11.000
better formatted answers, more entertaining answers

01:38:11.000 --> 01:38:14.000
but it is possible that the best model

01:38:14.000 --> 01:38:17.000
does not know all this

01:38:17.000 --> 01:38:20.000
then it will fail in the human leaderboard ranking

01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:23.000
so that is also a problem

01:38:23.000 --> 01:38:26.000
and lastly leaderboard scores are stale, incomplete

01:38:26.000 --> 01:38:29.000
and self-reported

