01:39:26.000 --> 01:39:29.000
that we have to discuss

01:39:29.000 --> 01:39:32.000
if you are an AI engineer

01:39:32.000 --> 01:39:35.000
and you are building an LLM based application

01:39:35.000 --> 01:39:38.000
and your first job is to select a model

01:39:38.000 --> 01:39:41.000
for that

01:39:41.000 --> 01:39:44.000
so how will you read LLM leaderboards

01:39:44.000 --> 01:39:47.000
so this is a guideline

01:39:47.000 --> 01:39:50.000
which I will tell you quickly

01:39:50.000 --> 01:39:53.000
before going on any leaderboard

01:39:53.000 --> 01:39:56.000
first of all you have to clear these 4-5 things in your mind

01:39:56.000 --> 01:39:59.000
what type of application are you making

01:39:59.000 --> 01:40:02.000
how much latency do you need in that application

01:40:02.000 --> 01:40:05.000
how much cost can you bear

01:40:05.000 --> 01:40:08.000
what are the needs of your context

01:40:08.000 --> 01:40:11.000
are there any deployment constraints

01:40:11.000 --> 01:40:14.000
can I use general publicly available models

01:40:14.000 --> 01:40:17.000
or I have to use an on-premise model

01:40:17.000 --> 01:40:20.000
so write all these things clearly

01:40:20.000 --> 01:40:23.000
if this becomes clear then you will not be biased

01:40:23.000 --> 01:40:26.000
towards the rank 1 model

01:40:26.000 --> 01:40:29.000
if I want to set up an on-premise

01:40:29.000 --> 01:40:32.000
then obviously I cannot use Claude Fayble

01:40:32.000 --> 01:40:35.000
then I will have to go towards open source

01:40:35.000 --> 01:40:38.000
so that opens up my mind

01:40:38.000 --> 01:40:41.000
in step 2 you will not blindly go to any leaderboard

01:40:41.000 --> 01:40:44.000
you will go to the leaderboard which is related to your work

01:40:44.000 --> 01:40:47.000
if you are making an agent

01:40:47.000 --> 01:40:50.000
then you will go to an agent related leaderboard

01:40:50.000 --> 01:40:53.000
if you are making a chatbot

01:40:53.000 --> 01:40:56.000
then you will go to the leaderboard

01:40:56.000 --> 01:40:59.000
because you are getting direct objective measurement of the chatting

01:40:59.000 --> 01:41:02.000
if you are making a rag then you will go to MTB

01:41:02.000 --> 01:41:05.000
this is actually the leaderboard of the ranking of the embedding models

01:41:05.000 --> 01:41:08.000
you have to put an embedding model in the rag

01:41:08.000 --> 01:41:11.000
there are budget constraints in your application

01:41:11.000 --> 01:41:14.000
so you will probably go to the artificial analysis

01:41:14.000 --> 01:41:17.000
or Vellum leaderboard

01:41:17.000 --> 01:41:20.000
because here you get exact information

01:41:20.000 --> 01:41:23.000
about the speed of the embedding models

01:41:23.000 --> 01:41:26.000
and how much money is spent

01:41:26.000 --> 01:41:29.000
so this is step 2, you go to the right leaderboard

01:41:29.000 --> 01:41:32.000
after that read the leaderboard correctly

01:41:32.000 --> 01:41:35.000
means you understand everything

01:41:35.000 --> 01:41:38.000
what is being scored, how is it being scored

01:41:38.000 --> 01:41:41.000
who has evaluated it, how much was the inference budget

01:41:41.000 --> 01:41:44.000
is there any reasoning or not

01:41:44.000 --> 01:41:47.000
when is the evaluation data set, how old is it

01:41:47.000 --> 01:41:50.000
is it being updated, is a test set being maintained

01:41:50.000 --> 01:41:53.000
or not

01:41:53.000 --> 01:41:56.000
have the benchmarks been saturated or not

01:41:56.000 --> 01:41:59.000
confidence interval, we discussed

01:41:59.000 --> 01:42:02.000
if it is a small data set then how much is the confidence interval

01:42:02.000 --> 01:42:05.000
has it been told or not, if not then

01:42:05.000 --> 01:42:08.000
then two very close models are effectively the same

01:42:08.000 --> 01:42:11.000
if the composite leaderboard is adding multiple benchmarks

01:42:11.000 --> 01:42:14.000
and giving a score then what are the

01:42:14.000 --> 01:42:17.000
values and weightage, you will analyze all this

01:42:17.000 --> 01:42:20.000
here are just many pointers written but you get the idea

01:42:20.000 --> 01:42:23.000
blindly there is a number, you don't have to trust it

